Z||000000||DisfourDan||07-07-2001||01:46 AM||||Here in Antioch, CA, there are not many Fieros, only about 4 that I have seen.  Out of those 4, 3 of them are in terrible condition.  Just wondering, is there a reason why people  (excluding Fiero lovers) do not take care of there Fieros?  <P>Im guessing they get a there daily excitement by driving a Fiero that is about to blow up.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-07-2001||02:32 AM||||It's because they are inexpensive cars usually given to teenagers as a first car to beat on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||07-07-2001||08:07 AM||||People don't take care of cars in general. People realize that they are a mechanical device with a set lifespan and that mechanics are expensive and upset the cost/value balance of keeping an older car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Elvira||07-07-2001||08:40 AM||||I know what you mean.  Chester and I just picked up an 87Blue GT 5Speed that had been given to a 16 year old kid (no disrespect to teens as I have 2 myself) who really used and abused the car.  Of course the father who we bought the car from was Fiero illiterate.  He had just picked up a blue notchback in almost perfect condition for $250 and was like "Well I didn't want it but the price was right so I'll just use it as my daily driver, no big deal".  We are trying to get him to sell it (hopefully before he trashes it).<P>------------------<BR>Elvira<BR>Blue 87GT<BR>Black 86GT 5sp x 2<BR>86SC t-top 58k miles||||reg||1
